    "description": "Iran’s theater festivals provide distinct settings for stage productions, street performance, puppetry, and work for young audiences. Their selection procedures, competitive sections, and geographical focus vary, so each edition must be understood through its own program and documentation.",
    "tags": ["Theater festivals", "Fadjr", "Puppetry", "Street theater", "Children's theater", "Regional arts"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T03:41:12Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T03:41:12Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Theater Festivals in Iran: Formats, Places, and Evidence | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Fadjr, Tehran-Mobarak, Hamedan, and Marivan through their different audiences, performance settings, and documented festival programs."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "different-platforms",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A network of different platforms",
        "content": "Theater festivals in Iran are not a single interchangeable circuit. Fadjr offers a broad platform, Tehran-Mobarak specializes in puppetry, Hamedan focuses on children and young people, and Marivan is associated with street theater. These distinctions affect the setting of a performance, the audience it addresses, and the professional networks around it. [s1][s3][s4][s5]\n\nA festival call explains what organizers want to present. A published program records scheduled work, while a closing report provides evidence of completed activity. Keeping these documents separate prevents an invitation, application total, or promotional statement from becoming an unsupported account of what audiences actually saw. The sources below illustrate why that distinction matters. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "fadjr",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Fadjr and the importance of sections",
        "content": "The call for the forty-third Fadjr International Theatre Festival, reproduced by the ECO Cultural Institute, announced stage and outdoor performances in Tehran for January 21–February 2, 2025. It described the international section as noncompetitive, while also announcing a festival director’s prize. These provisions show why a single label such as competitive can be misleading without identifying the section and award mechanism. [s1]\n\nA subsequent iFilm report confirmed a closing ceremony at Vahdat Hall and named awards in stage and street theater. It identified Malek Mohammad, directed by Sahand and Babak Qeisari from Tabriz, as a stage-section special jury award recipient. This documents recognition of a production from outside Tehran, although one award cannot establish the national distribution of opportunities. The report’s reference to works received internationally should likewise not be read as a count of performances staged. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "specialist-regional",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Specialist audiences and places",
        "content": "Tehran-Mobarak’s announced twenty-first edition was framed as a noncompetitive meeting place for traditional and contemporary puppetry. Its July 2026 call gave October 18–23 as the intended dates. This is evidence of the organizers’ stated scope; the call alone does not establish the final program or completion of the event. [s3]\n\nHamedan’s role is different. ASSITEJ International, the international association concerned with theater for children and young people, circulated a call for the thirty-first International Children and Youth Theatre Festival. It announced November 29–December 4, 2026, in Hamedan Province. Those dates fall after this page’s research cutoff and are presented as plans. [s4]\n\nMarivan, in Kurdistan Province in western Iran, offers another setting. Rudaw’s October 2025 reporting described its eighteenth International Street Theater Festival as underway, with performers using the city as a public performance space. Interviews with an organizer and a returning participant emphasized cultural connection and the experience of returning to the festival. Those accounts convey participants’ perspectives rather than independently measured social outcomes. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-programs",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How to interpret a festival listing",
        "content": "For readers exploring this field, the most useful questions are specific: Is the work indoors or outdoors? Is it intended for children, adults, or mixed audiences? Does international describe invited productions, submitted applications, or professional guests? Is an award decided by a jury or conferred separately by the director? The inspected calls and reports use these categories differently. Reading them precisely gives a clearer picture of artistic activity than treating edition numbers or promotional claims as a ranking of importance. [s1][s2][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
